1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,400
Equal time steps, but growing gaps. That is constant acceleration.

2
00:00:04,766 --> 00:00:07,166
The four kinematics equations for constant acceleration.

3
00:00:08,666 --> 00:00:11,666
Most classes hand you these four and say: memorize them.

4
00:00:12,066 --> 00:00:14,266
But where do they come from?

5
00:00:14,266 --> 00:00:16,866
We will derive all four using nothing but algebra.

6
00:00:18,666 --> 00:00:21,666
An object starts at velocity v-naught and ends at velocity v.

7
00:00:20,366 --> 00:00:22,966
In between, a steady acceleration a, over an elapsed time t.

8
00:00:23,466 --> 00:00:26,266
Five quantities in total, and every equation links them.

9
00:00:26,066 --> 00:00:29,466
Constant acceleration means the velocity changes by the same amount every second.

10
00:00:32,666 --> 00:00:35,666
Because the acceleration never changes, the velocity graph is a straight line.

11
00:00:35,866 --> 00:00:38,866
Acceleration is the change in velocity divided by the change in time.

12
00:00:37,666 --> 00:00:40,466
The velocity changes from v-naught to v, over a time t.

13
00:00:39,566 --> 00:00:41,566
Multiply both sides by t.

14
00:00:41,266 --> 00:00:44,266
Add v-naught to both sides, and there is equation one.

15
00:00:46,366 --> 00:00:49,766
Look at its shape: y equals m x plus b. Intercept v-naught, slope a.

16
00:00:53,066 --> 00:00:56,266
If the velocity never changed, displacement is simply velocity times time.

17
00:00:56,666 --> 00:00:59,466
But the velocity does change. So what do we use instead?

18
00:00:58,566 --> 00:01:01,966
Displacement equals average velocity times time. That is not an approximation.

19
00:01:01,466 --> 00:01:04,066
So what is the average of a straight line?

20
00:01:04,066 --> 00:01:06,666
Try the midpoint of the two end velocities.

21
00:01:05,266 --> 00:01:07,866
In the first half the object is slower than that midpoint.

22
00:01:06,266 --> 00:01:09,066
In the second half it is faster, by exactly the same shape.

23
00:01:08,066 --> 00:01:11,266
Rotate the surplus half a turn and it fills the deficit perfectly.

24
00:01:11,466 --> 00:01:15,066
Every moment that is too slow is matched by one exactly as much too fast.

25
00:01:16,366 --> 00:01:19,166
So the average velocity is the midpoint of the two ends.

26
00:01:18,266 --> 00:01:21,066
Put that back in, and there is equation two.

27
00:01:25,266 --> 00:01:28,866
Equation two still mentions the final velocity. Replace it using equation one.

28
00:01:27,666 --> 00:01:30,266
Substitute v-naught plus a t in place of v.

29
00:01:29,366 --> 00:01:31,966
The two v-naught terms collect into two v-naught.

30
00:01:31,166 --> 00:01:33,766
Halve everything inside the bracket.

31
00:01:32,966 --> 00:01:35,566
Distribute the t, and there is equation three.

32
00:01:39,766 --> 00:01:42,766
The rectangle is how far you would go if you never sped up.

33
00:01:41,766 --> 00:01:44,766
The triangle is the extra distance the acceleration bought you.

34
00:01:43,766 --> 00:01:46,566
Two terms in the equation, two pieces in the picture.

35
00:01:48,366 --> 00:01:51,366
Sometimes time is the one thing you do not know.

36
00:01:51,766 --> 00:01:54,566
Take equation one and solve it for t.

37
00:01:56,566 --> 00:01:58,966
Now put that into equation two.

38
00:02:00,266 --> 00:02:03,466
Look at that numerator: v plus v-naught, times v minus v-naught.

39
00:02:01,466 --> 00:02:03,866
That is a difference of squares.

40
00:02:05,466 --> 00:02:08,466
So the numerator collapses to v squared minus v-naught squared.

41
00:02:07,466 --> 00:02:09,666
Multiply both sides by two a.

42
00:02:09,166 --> 00:02:11,766
Rearrange, and there is equation four.

43
00:02:12,066 --> 00:02:14,666
Notice there is no t in it anywhere.

44
00:02:16,666 --> 00:02:20,666
A car starts from rest and accelerates at three metres per second squared for five seconds.

45
00:02:19,466 --> 00:02:22,466
We know v-naught, a, and t. We want the displacement.

46
00:02:22,066 --> 00:02:25,466
We do not know the final velocity, so use the equation that leaves it out.

47
00:02:24,366 --> 00:02:27,166
That is equation three. Plug in the numbers.

48
00:02:26,566 --> 00:02:28,966
Thirty-seven and a half metres.

49
00:02:30,966 --> 00:02:34,166
Equation one gives the final velocity: fifteen metres per second.

50
00:02:33,966 --> 00:02:37,166
Feed that into equation two, and it gives the same displacement.

51
00:02:36,366 --> 00:02:39,566
They agree, because all four came from the same straight line.

52
00:02:41,766 --> 00:02:45,766
Four equations, and each one leaves out exactly one of the five quantities.

53
00:02:46,366 --> 00:02:49,966
Find the quantity you neither know nor want, and the equation picks itself.

54
00:02:50,866 --> 00:02:55,866
Two ideas did all the work: acceleration is the slope of the line, and displacement is its average height times the width.
